cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use or reference LYSPYTHON in your academic research, please cite it as below."
title: "LYSPYTHON: A Lisp-Macro-Defined Python Runtime & REPL"
authors:
  - family-names: "LYSPYTHON Contributors"
    given-names: "Research Engineering Team"
    affiliation: "Programming Language Systems Research Group"
abstract: >
  LYSPYTHON is a meta-language runtime architecture that inverts the traditional
  relationship between Common Lisp and Python. Rather than embedding Lisp within
  Python or invoking foreign CPython procedures from Lisp, LYSPYTHON establishes
  Common Lisp as an active meta-linguistic definition layer. User-written Python
  surface syntax is ingested into a structured surface abstract syntax tree (AST),
  transformed and desugared via user-extensible Common Lisp macros, lowered into a
  canonical intermediate AST, and evaluated on a standard CPython execution engine
  via C-API bindings. This architecture permits live, interactive redefinition of
  imperative syntax, semantics, and control structures at runtime.
repository-code: "https://github.com/samudragupto/lys-python-runtime"
url: "https://github.com/samudragupto/lys-python-runtime"
type: software
license: Apache-2.0
version: "0.1.0-alpha"
date-released: "2026-10-02"
keywords:
  - "programming-languages"
  - "macro-systems"
  - "common-lisp"
  - "python-runtime"
  - "metaprogramming"
  - "language-workbenches"
  - "ast-transformations"
  - "compiler-architecture"
  - "repl"
